Actual results:

In Firefox, you get the following text in the log:
<TABLE> [offsetTop = 8px] [parent's clientTop = 0px] [scrollTop = 0px]
Accumulated offset to ICB: 8px
gBCR()-based offset to ICB: 8px
---
<TD> [offsetTop = 2px] [parent's clientTop = 0px] [scrollTop = 0px]
Accumulated offset to ICB: 10px
gBCR()-based offset to ICB: 79px
---
<SPAN> [offsetTop = 1px] [parent's clientTop = 1px] [scrollTop = 0px]
Accumulated offset to ICB: 12px
gBCR()-based offset to ICB: 81px

The .offsetTop value for the <td> is wrong. It appears to be reporting the distance between the td's border edge and the *table grid box's* content edge (the table grid box just contains the rows; it omits the caption). This means that when you sum the offsetTops and clientTops up the offsetParent chain, you get the wrong answer.


Expected results:

In Chrome, you get the following:
<TABLE> [offsetTop = 8px] [parent's clientTop = 0px] [scrollTop = 0px]
Accumulated offset to ICB: 8px
gBCR()-based offset to ICB: 8px
---
<TD> [offsetTop = 58px] [parent's clientTop = 0px] [scrollTop = 0px]
Accumulated offset to ICB: 66px
gBCR()-based offset to ICB: 66px
---
<SPAN> [offsetTop = 1px] [parent's clientTop = 1px] [scrollTop = 0px]
Accumulated offset to ICB: 68px
gBCR()-based offset to ICB: 68px

The "accumulated offset" and "gBCR()-based offset" agrees on every element, and in particular, the <td>'s offsetTop is a large value, clearly relative to the content box of the *table wrapper box*, taking into account the amount of space occupied by the <caption> box.

Per spec <https://drafts.csswg.org/cssom-view/#dom-htmlelement-offsettop>, offsetTop needs to return the distance between the elements' border box and its offsetParent's content box. .clientTop then gives the distance from an element's content box to its own border box. Summing these up the .offsetParent chain should give the total offset from the ICB down to the element, which you can use for positioning other things. (You have to adjust for scrollers as well, which isn't relevant in the provided example code, but still handled by the function in case you want to construct a more complex example.)

